WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.780
Start with the explanation you want students to construct.

2
00:00:03.980 --> 00:00:07.527
WriteSplat helps develop and format the text.

3
00:00:07.727 --> 00:00:11.452
ShowSplat sequences an explanation for presentation.

4
00:00:11.652 --> 00:00:14.857
LayoutSplat designs a publication or ebook.

5
00:00:15.057 --> 00:00:20.553
These are different communication forms, not a checklist that every learner must complete.

6
00:00:20.753 --> 00:00:28.147
PlanSplat can organize the work, and MarkdownSplat can preserve structured source text when that format is useful.

7
00:00:28.347 --> 00:00:35.027
For our common example, draft a short claim, cite two observations and explain a limitation.

8
00:00:35.227 --> 00:00:39.811
A three-slide version can show the question, evidence and recommendation.

9
00:00:40.011 --> 00:00:44.637
A one-page guide can use the same reasoning with a chart and text summary.

10
00:00:44.837 --> 00:00:48.214
Avoid copying a dense paragraph onto every slide.

11
00:00:48.414 --> 00:00:52.069
Give the audience one clear purpose for each visual moment.

12
00:00:52.269 --> 00:00:55.704
Keep the editable source as well as the finished export.

13
00:00:55.904 --> 00:01:02.972
Import and export conversions may change layout, fonts or other features, so inspect the resulting file.

14
00:01:03.172 --> 00:01:08.908
PDFsplat can help annotate, organize or present a PDF when that suits the lesson.

15
00:01:09.108 --> 00:01:16.225
Before you share, check the reading sequence, image descriptions and whether the document still communicates the

16
00:01:16.425 --> 00:01:18.649
main reasoning without your narration.

